<!--
  The themed-icon layer: the same "K" wordmark as `ic_launcher_foreground`, drawn as one solid
  silhouette.

  Android's themed icons (13+) take this drawable's **alpha only** and fill it with one colour from the
  wallpaper's scheme, over a tonal background it generates. Both layers used to be the same file, which
  is the reason for this one — not because a tint flattens the artwork's colours, since the foreground
  is a single flat #FFFFFF and comes through a tint unchanged. Two narrower reasons:

  1. **The page fan does not survive the size a launcher draws.** The brand foreground builds the book's
     edge out of three hairline slivers of negative space, about half a unit wide in a 108-unit
     viewport. At the 48dp a home screen actually renders, that is a fifth of a pixel: the strokes stop
     being lines and become a smear of half-lit grey along the mark's lower arm. Here that whole
     passage is one straight edge, so the arm is a solid wedge and the mark stays crisp all the way
     down to 36dp. The small page curl above the stem is kept — it is an order of magnitude heavier
     than the fan and still reads.
  2. **The two layers want to be able to differ.** While `<monochrome>` pointed at the colour
     foreground, any later change to the brand art — a gradient, a second tone, a highlight — would
     have silently become a monochrome bug, because *that* is the case where a flat tint destroys
     information. Splitting them now means the brand icon can be drawn freely and this one stays a
     silhouette by construction.

  The geometry is the foreground's own, so the two cannot drift apart in size or position: same 108dp
  viewport, same path, and the same outer radius of 30.75 units from the centre — inside the 33-unit
  (66dp) safe circle every adaptive layer has to respect, with room for Samsung's zoom.
-->
